; Editing-request tests: the seven requests the controller routes to the
; engine's editing features, layer 2 of the test design. Each `.t` drives the
; built server through the test client over pipes, so every test runs the real
; binary and the record is the protocol's own bytes, normalised.
;
; What these tests pin is the routing and the empty answer, not what the
; engine computes: the engine's own answers are its lane's tests. A routed
; request is no longer MethodNotFound, so a client that asks before the
; engine can answer sees the empty answer its request has in the protocol
; (null, or an empty list) and never an error, except for rename, which has
; no empty answer and is refused with the engine's reason.
;
; The two executables are declared as dependencies even though dune would
; build them anyway: the dependency is what makes a rebuilt server or client
; re-run these tests instead of leaving a recorded pass from the previous
; binary in place. A fixture a test reads that is not listed here would not
; invalidate its record, so a fixture added later belongs in this list too.
;
; The stanza must never be given `(files ...)`, `(applies_to ...)` or
; `(enabled_if false)`: each of those leaves a `.t` file in the tree with no
; record, which `scripts/runtest.sh` refuses.

(cram
 (deps
  %{exe:../../client/jide_test_client.exe}
  %{exe:../../../lsp-server/native/main.exe}))
